Z||000014||beer_stud_21||04-20-2001||09:52 PM||||N_Man,<P>Look on all the chips to see if the speed rating is printed on them.  The fastest kind is 2-2-2 speed.  Im guessing your old ram is 3-2-2 or 3-2-3.  I added a 256mb chip to my iMac DV SE the other day and have been getting some odd errors.  The chip I added was 2-2-2 speed and the existing 128 was 3-2-2 speed.  I have yet to troubleshoot the problems Ive been having to see if they are software or bad ram/ bad ram mix like you had.  If one of the 2 chips you pulled is the same speed at your new 128meg chip try putting it in and see if your errors come back.  My guess is they wont.<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000015||Formula88||04-20-2001||10:48 PM||||It sounds like you may have missmatched memory.  You can try slowing down the memory timing in BIOS to see if that helps.  If your new memory is faster than the old, and your PC is set for the new speed, then you will get errors.  You'll have to run all the memory at the speed of the slowest installed stick.||||reg||1
Z||000016||BC-GT||04-21-2001||12:12 AM||||Knowing what type of motherboard and memory you are running is the first order of the day? Some of the earlier Abit boards were notoriously terrible with even more then 1 stick of memory at a time. Are you overclocking anything in the system?<BR>I 've gotten those types of errors with overclocking(registry and kernel errors)Two other areas I would check are the fan on your CPU and your main power supply. Also, I've been running WinMe since it came out and its been rock stable for me, can't seem to bring it down anyhow. it's almost always a poorly written app. or hardware driver that cause all the grief, people forget that when they crap all over Microsoft and its millions of apps written for it.<BR> Lastly you might want to post a note to one of the hardware forums such as the one on Anandtech.com or overclockers.com etc. etc.<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||N_Man||04-21-2001||02:41 AM||||Ok the old memory is pc100 the new stuff is pc133, motherboard is ABIT BH6  runs overclocked at 126mhz, been stable at that speed for about 6 months.  when I got the new ram i specifically asked about my situation and the guy at the store (most reputable one in the city) said do the pc133 (same price) because it should be more stable.<P>Ive since figured out how to extract specific files from the windows cd, havent done it yet but ill give it a shot tomorrow.<P>Yes all the memory should theoretaclly run at the speed of the slowest stick, which if all works well is running at the speed of the BIOS, so using pc133 ram on a 100mhz overclocked to 126 mhz should be better than buying a 100mhz stick and hoping that it works.<P>But what is stumping me is why my old ram died when i put the new stuff in.  even if i set my BIOS back to 100mhz the ram still decides to give me errors.  Any thoughts on that?||||reg||1
