Z||000019||LarryB||04-21-2001||06:58 AM||||A couple of you guys touched on the potential problem.<P>If you're running an Abit BH6 at 124 MHz FSB (I've never seen a BH6 with a 126MHz FSB setting) you're running the PCI bus at ~41.3 MHz.  That's a pretty serious bus overclock, a lot of cards can't handle that speed, and you can have trouble with the IDE controllers as well.  Some manufacturers hard drives (Maxtor is one) have a reputation for not handling overclocking of the IDE bus very well.<P>The BH-6 doesn't buffer the memory slots either, it improves stability and is a nice-to-have but it also increases the cost of the board and with only 3 memory slots its generally not a problem although it can be less tolerant of certain memory or if you use multiple sticks of memory. <P>The BH-6 is a good motherboard (I've had 3 systems built with them before) but it can be quirky if you're overclocking using generic components.<P>So - there are 4 steps you can try for starters:<P> - Take that 128MB stick of RAM and try seating it in each of the different memory slots.  Sometimes a BH6 is unstable with memory in the first slot but fine when the stick is in another slot.  <P>- If you still have trouble remove the 128MB stick and reinsert the old memory.<P>- Go back to a 100MHz FSB.  (This is the first rule of thumb when you have trouble with an overclocked system - see if the problem still occurs at the default setting)<P>- My guess is that you bought a stick of generic memory or something like PNY or Kingstons "value" line.  Take it back and order a stick of 128 or 256MB CAS2 memory from <A HREF="http://www.crucial.com." TARGET=_blank>http://www.crucial.com.</A>   Mushkin memory has a great reputation as well but its usually quite a bit more expensive than Crucial. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||btoth||04-21-2001||09:53 AM||||Don't mix 133Mhz memory with 100Mhz memory... they're clocked for your bus speed.  Even if your overclocking use what you're supposed to use with the motherboard.  Return the 128MB 133Mhz and get a 128MB 100Mhz and it should work just fine.<P>Maybe it's the overclocking making things unstable.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Loki||04-21-2001||10:13 AM||||Well after speaking with my friend at microsoft he says ME *cough SUCKS cough*  Win 98 or go win2k<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>www.cox-internet.com/fiero  !!!UPDATED!!!||||reg||1
Z||000022||Mach10||04-21-2001||06:24 PM||||you already have the ram, don't sell it, keep it for later. Go to your BIOS, set the CAS settings to 3 (precharge, delay) and set the paging to slow. That should even things out. Oh, also, move the 128mb DIMM to slot 0 (or 1, the first one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).||||reg||6
